.TH PKG 1
.SH NAME
pkg \- namespace-native package manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pkg pack
.I stagedir
.I out.tar
.br
.B pkg genkey
.I owner
.I skfile
.RI [ bits ]
.br
.B pkg sign
.I pkg.tar
.I skfile
.br
.B pkg verify
.RB [ -k
.IR pkfile ]
.I pkg.tar
.br
.B pkg info
.I pkg.tar
.br
.B pkg install
.RB [ -k
.IR pkfile ]
.I pkg.tar
.br
.B pkg list
.br
.B pkg remove
.I name
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pkg
installs software packages by composing them into the name space rather
than copying files into a shared tree.
A package is a
.IR tar (1)
archive containing a
.B pkg/manifest
file and any of the
.BR dis/ ,
.B man/
and
.B lib/
directories.
The manifest is a set of
.IR attribute " " value
lines, one per line, in the
.IR ndb (6)
style; blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored:
.EX

	name util/pkgdemo
	version 1.0
	category util
	keywords demo date example
	provides pkgdemo
	description a demo package

.EE
.PP
Installing a package does not unpack it or touch any shared directory.
The archive is mounted read-only with
.IR tarfs (4)
at
.BI /mnt/pkg/ id\fR,
where
.I id
is the first 16 hex digits of the SHA-256 of the archive, and the
package's
.B dis/
directory (and
.BR man/ ,
if present) is bound after the current contents of
.B /dis
(and
.BR /man ).
The package's commands then resolve by bare name.
Because installation is a name-space operation it needs no special
privilege, affects only the invoking name space, and is undone exactly by
unbinding and unmounting.
Each install is named by the content hash of its archive, so installing
the same bytes twice is idempotent.
.SS Signing
A package may carry a detached signature in
.IB pkg.tar .sig
that authenticates the archive's exact bytes.
.B pkg genkey
writes an RSA secret key to
.I skfile
and the matching public key to
.IB skfile .pk
.RI ( bits
defaults to 1024).
.B pkg sign
hashes
.I pkg.tar
and writes
.IB pkg.tar .sig,
a
.IR keyring (2)
certificate over that hash together with the signer's public key.
.B pkg verify
checks that signature against the archive; with
.B -k
it also requires the signer to be the key in
.IR pkfile .
.B pkg install
verifies the signature when a
.B .sig
is present, rejects a package whose signer does not match a
.BR -k pkfile,
and warns when installing an unsigned package.
Signing uses the same
.IR keyring (2)
key and certificate types as
.IR v23-security (2)
principals, so a later milestone can check a publisher's blessing chain
rather than a single pinned key.
.SS Commands
.TP
.B pack
Build
.I out.tar
from
.IR stagedir ,
which must contain a
.B pkg/manifest
and whichever of
.BR dis/ ,
.BR man/ ,
.B lib/
apply.
.TP
.B genkey
Generate a signing keypair for
.IR owner .
.TP
.B sign
Sign
.I pkg.tar
with the secret key in
.IR skfile .
.TP
.B verify
Check the signature on
.IR pkg.tar ,
optionally requiring signer
.IR pkfile .
.TP
.B info
Print the content SHA-256 and the manifest of
.I pkg.tar
without installing it.
.TP
.B install
Verify the signature (if any), then mount and bind
.I pkg.tar
into the name space as described above.
With
.B -k
the package must be signed by
.IR pkfile .
.TP
.B list
List the installed packages \(em name, version and content id \(em read
from the packages actually mounted under
.BR /mnt/pkg .
.TP
.B remove
Unbind and unmount every installed package whose manifest
.B name
is
.IR name .
.SH EXAMPLES
Package, install, use and remove a tool:
.EX

	pkg pack stage util-pkgdemo.tar
	pkg install util-pkgdemo.tar
	pkgdemo
	pkg list
	pkg remove util/pkgdemo

.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/pkg.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tarfs (4),
.IR tar (1),
.IR bind (1),
.IR ndb (6)
.PP
.B /doc/pkg-roadmap.md
.SH BUGS
Local packages only (milestones 1\(en2): packages are named on the
command line, not fetched.
Trust is a single pinned key; a publisher blessing chain, a
.B /pkg
naming service, and
.B search
are later milestones.
An installed package keeps a
.IR tarfs (4)
server process alive for as long as it is mounted.
.SH HISTORY
.B Pkg
first appeared in Inferno-rio (2026).
